Description: hw/sensor: Add the Kionix KXP74 accelerometer
 The Chumby Classic's sensor card carries a Kionix KXP74, a three-axis
 analog accelerometer with a 12-bit ADC behind an SPI command protocol.
 The stock driver (drivers/char/chumby_accel.c, "2.1-Kionix-Ironforge")
 uses it like this, each step in one chip-select frame:
 .
 - enable: a 16-bit word 0x0404, the write-control command (0x04) and
   the control byte, bit 2 set (power on)
 - sample an axis: an 8-bit command, 0x00 for X, 0x02 for Y, 0x01 for Z;
   then, after 50 us, a 16-bit exchange whose reply holds the result in
   bits 15:4
 .
 It reports the samples through /dev/accel with a range of 2500 mg
 (GRANGE), 0 g at mid-scale.
 .
 Model that: the first byte of a frame is the command, the next two
 bytes of a conversion clock out the 12-bit result, MSB first,
 left-aligned; the byte after a write-control command sets the control
 register, and a disabled part converts to 0. The output is ratiometric
 around 2048 with 819.2 counts per g, so the ADC spans +/-2.5 g as the
 driver expects. Conversions finish at once. The part's datasheet was
 not at hand, so only the commands the stock driver sends are modelled.
 .
 The properties x-mg, y-mg and z-mg set the acceleration along each axis
 in milli-g and can be changed at run time (qom-set), so tilt and shake
 can be tested; a reset leaves them alone. The default is a part lying
 flat and still, +1 g on Z.
